# XCU more
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# more with standard output redirected to a file, which is the only mode a
# script can drive; the page-by-page mode needs a terminal and is recorded
# as untestable. Standard error is redirected to a file in every run, so the
# no-error-without-readable-stderr rule is exercised.

assertion  XCU.more.001
anchor     utilities/more.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/more.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/more.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       When standard output is not a terminal, the input file is copied
           to it in its entirety without modification, and options other
           than -s (here -c, -e, -i, -n, -u) have no effect, even with
           standard error unreadable.
test       t/001-copy-through.sh

assertion  XCU.more.002
anchor     utilities/more.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/more.html#STDIN
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       With no file operands standard input is copied; with several
           operands they are copied in order one after another; a - operand
           reads standard input at that point in the sequence.
test       t/002-operands-and-stdin.sh

assertion  XCU.more.003
anchor     utilities/more.html#OPTIONS
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       With -s runs of consecutive empty lines in the input come out as
           a single empty line and everything else is unchanged.
test       t/003-squeeze.sh

assertion  XCU.more.004
anchor     utilities/more.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       Options in the MORE variable act as if written before the command
           line options, so MORE=-s squeezes empty lines in the same way as
           -s.
test       t/004-more-variable.sh

assertion  XCU.more.005
anchor     utilities/more.html#EXIT STATUS
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       A file operand that cannot be read gives a non-zero exit status.
test       t/005-missing-file.sh

assertion  XCU.more.006
anchor     utilities/more.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/more.html#STDERR
class      untestable
since      1992
says       With standard output a terminal, the files are shown a screenful
           at a time under user commands, with a prompt on standard error,
           folding of long lines and the interactive commands.
reason     A terminal device and a person (or a terminal driver) are needed
           to see screens and type commands; a portable script has no way to
           create one.
